INFINIMINER
===========

Infiniminer is a team-based block mining and building game by Zach Barth (Zachtronics), released under
the MIT license.

This repository contains two versions of the source:

  src/   A modern, cross-platform port that runs on Windows, macOS and Linux (.NET 8 + MonoGame).
  code/  The original 2009 v1.5 source (Visual Studio 2008, XNA Game Studio 3.0, Windows only),
         kept for reference. The modern port loads its art and sounds from code/InfiniminerClient/Content.


RUNNING THE MODERN VERSION
==========================

The only requirement is the .NET 8 SDK (https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download). No content pipeline,
shader compiler, Visual Studio or Wine is needed on any platform.

    dotnet run --project src/Infiniminer.Server      # start a dedicated server
    dotnet run --project src/Infiniminer.Client      # start the game

No separate server is needed to play: [ HOST A GAME ] at the top of the server list starts one inside the
game (pick the world size, player limit, time limit and so on), and players on your network see it in
their server list. With KEEP WORLD on, ending the game saves it, and HOST GAME offers to CONTINUE it next
time (or start a NEW WORLD). Dedicated servers do the same with "persist = true". The in-game menu's END GAME stops it. A dedicated server is still the way to run a game
that outlives your session.

MAKING MAPS: host a game with SANDBOX on. In a sandbox, building is free, the construction gun also
places the world's own blocks (dirt, rock, ore, gold, diamonds, lava and metal), and everyone carries the
deconstruction gun, which takes away any block. When the map is ready, SAVE MAP in the in-game menu saves
it (to maps/<name>.map in your settings folder); MAP in HOST GAME then starts games on it, at its size,
in any mode. A dedicated server starts on one with "map = <name>" (its maps folder), and the console
commands "savemap <name>" and "loadmap <name>" save and load them while it runs.

To let friends on the internet join a game you host, set INTERNET PLAYERS to OPEN ROUTER (UPNP) in HOST
GAME (or "upnp = true" for a dedicated server). The game asks your router to forward its port and tells
you the address to give out (the in-game menu's INTERNET row copies it). If the router doesn't support it
or has it switched off, the game says so: forward UDP port 5565 to your computer by hand instead.

NEW TO THE GAME? Tips at the top of the screen walk you through it one step at a time (dig down, find ore,
build a ladder to climb out, find treasure, bank it), and the block under your crosshair is named and explained.
Each tip goes away once you've done it; TIPS FOR NEW PLAYERS in OPTIONS turns them off, or back on from the start.

The server list (SELECT A SERVER) shows the games on your LAN and, if a public server list is set, on the
internet, as a table: mode, world size, players, computer players and ping, with a line about the selected game.
It keeps itself up to date, dims full servers and marks ones running another version. QUICK PLAY joins the
best open game (people playing, then nearest), or starts one with computer players if there isn't one. It
also lists the last five servers you joined (so a server joined by address is one click away next time). When a match ends and the server restarts with a
new map, the game rejoins it by itself. Use [ DIRECT CONNECT ] for anything else; it accepts
an IP address or host name, optionally followed by ":port".

To build release packages (self-contained: players don't need .NET installed) into dist/:

    packaging/package.sh win-x64      # portable zip + installer (the installer needs NSIS: apt install nsis)
    packaging/package.sh linux-x64    # tar.gz (run install-desktop-entry.sh inside it to add a menu entry)
                                      # and an AppImage ("Infiniminer.AppImage server" runs the server)
    packaging/package.sh osx-arm64    # zip with Infiniminer.app (Apple Silicon); osx-x64 for Intel Macs.
                                      # Build these on a Mac so the app gets signed.
    packaging/flatpak/build.sh        # a .flatpak bundle (needs flatpak and flatpak-builder); install it with
                                      # "flatpak install --user dist/Infiniminer-*.flatpak", then
                                      # "flatpak run io.github.cybermobile.Infiniminer" (add
                                      # "--command=infiniminer-server" for the server). CI builds it and checks
                                      # the game and server start; not yet tried on a real desktop.

Each also builds the server list service for that platform. GitHub Actions (.github/workflows/build.yml)
builds and tests on all three operating systems, packages every platform and builds the Docker images on
each push. Pushing a version tag (e.g. v1.9.0) also creates a GitHub release with the packages attached
and publishes the images to ghcr.io.

Docker images for the dedicated server and the server list (see the top of each file for options):

    docker build -f docker/server.Dockerfile -t infiniminer-server .
    docker run -it -p 5565:5565/udp -v infiniminer:/data infiniminer-server

    docker build -f docker/serverlist.Dockerfile -t infiniminer-serverlist .
    docker run -p 8080:8080 infiniminer-serverlist

Linux notes: the game needs OpenGL drivers. Pasting into the direct connect box (Ctrl-V) needs the
xsel package.
macOS notes: the app is signed ad hoc, not by an Apple developer account, so the first time, right-click
Infiniminer.app and choose Open (or run `xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine Infiniminer.app`).


PUBLIC SERVER LIST
==================

The original public server list (apps.keithholman.net) is gone. src/Infiniminer.ServerList is a
replacement that anyone can host:

    dotnet run --project src/Infiniminer.ServerList -- --urls http://0.0.0.0:8080

Game servers with "public = true" and "serverlist = http://your-host:8080" announce themselves every five
minutes, and clients with the same serverlist setting show them in the server browser. Servers that stop
announcing disappear after 12 minutes. Opening the URL in a web browser shows the current list. The
address listed is the one the announcement came from; set TRUST_FORWARDED_HEADERS=true when running
behind a reverse proxy.


CONFIGURATION
=============

Game settings can be changed in the OPTIONS menu (on the server browser, or from the in-game menu): name,
resolution, fullscreen, interface scale, field of view, mouse and controller sensitivity, invert look,
controller vibration, volume, bloom, colour-blind mode, FPS counter and key bindings. The window can also be
resized freely; menus scale to fit it. In game, Escape on its own opens a menu (resume, change team or
class, options, respawn, leave server); Escape+Y, Escape+K and Escape+O still work as shortcuts.

SPECTATING: choose SPECTATE on the team screen to watch without playing. Fly with the movement keys (or the
left stick), Space/Shift (A/B) to go up and down; Tab (or Back) shows the scoreboard with who is watching.
Change team from the menu (or the change-team key) to join in. Spectators don't count towards team sizes.

CONTROLLERS: Xbox, PlayStation, Switch Pro and most other controllers work (buttons are named after the
Xbox ones; A is Cross on a PlayStation controller). Every menu works with the D-pad or left stick, A and B.
In game:

    Left stick   move                      Right stick  look
    A            jump / respawn            RT           use tool (hold to dig)
    RB, D-pad up/down     change tool      LB, D-pad left/right   change block
    X            deposit ore (at a bank)   B            withdraw ore (at a bank)
    Y            ping your team            Back (hold)  scoreboard
    LT + D-pad   quick team chat           Start        menu

Wherever text is needed (your name, a server address, chat), an on-screen keyboard appears for controller
players: D-pad or stick to move, A to type, B to delete, X for a space, Start when done, Back to cancel. In
game, click the right stick to chat to everyone or the left stick to chat to your team; in the server list,
Back types an address. LT + D-pad sends NEED ORE!, FOUND GOLD!, HELP ME! or ENEMY SPOTTED! to your team.

STEAM DECK: download the Linux AppImage in Desktop Mode, make it executable (right-click > Properties >
Permissions), and add it to Steam with "Add a Non-Steam Game". In Game Mode, choose a gamepad layout for it
(the "Gamepad" template, which presents the Deck's controls as an Xbox controller) and play with the controls
above; the first run opens at the Deck's 1280x800, which Game Mode shows full screen. Untested on a real Deck
so far. On-screen prompts switch between keyboard and controller buttons depending on
which you used last.

Settings are saved in client.config.txt; the server reads server.config.txt and keeps banlist.txt and
saved maps beside it. The defaults are in bin/ and are copied next to the executables on build. These
files are used from, in order:
  1. the current directory, if it has the config file;
  2. the folder of the executable, if it can be written to (an unzipped download or a dev build);
  3. otherwise (an installed copy) a per-user folder, seeded with the defaults on first run:
     %APPDATA%\Infiniminer on Windows, ~/Library/Application Support/Infiniminer on macOS,
     ~/.config/Infiniminer on Linux.

Other new settings:

  port        (server) UDP port to listen on, default 5565. LAN discovery only looks on 5565.
  serverlist  (client and server) URL of a public server list (see PUBLIC SERVER LIST). Empty by
              default, so only LAN discovery and direct connect are used.
  homebases   (server) give each team a home base to spawn at (default false).
  shocktouch  (server) shock blocks kill on any touch, not just from below (default false).
  timelimit   (server) match length in minutes; the richer team wins, ties go to overtime (default 0, off).
  gamemode    (server) "cash" (the original race to bank treasure, the default) or "hill": king of the hill.
              A metal platform in the middle of the map, with ramps up to it, earns $25 a second for the
              team with more players standing on it, on top of what they bank; bots go and hold it too.
              Also GAME MODE in HOST GAME.
  winningcashperplayer  (server) extra cash needed to win per connected player (default 0).
  autobalance (server) players can't join a team that already has more players (default false).
  mapsize     (server) size of the world cube: 64 (original), 96 or 128 (default 64).
  debuglatency (server) milliseconds of simulated network latency, for testing (default 0).

Fonts: the original used the 04b08 and 04b03b bitmap fonts by Yuji Oshimoto, which are not
redistributed here (http://www.dsg4.com/04/extra/bitmap/). The game ships with the look-alike
Silkscreen font (SIL Open Font License). To use the originals, put 04B_08__.TTF and 04B_03B_.TTF in
src/Infiniminer.Client/Fonts/ (or Content/fonts/ next to the built game).

Computer players: "bots = <count>" in server.config.txt, the "bots <count>" console command, or COMPUTER
PLAYERS in HOST GAME (5 by default, so three a side with you; it shows the teams each number makes). Bots dig down, tunnel and climb back to the surface to bank what they find, under
the same rules as everyone else, and come in every class: miners dig for gold and diamonds, prospectors
sense them from further away, engineers gather ore, climb out on ladders they build (which anyone can
use), build the team a bank if it has none, fence it in with the team's force fields (which only the
team can walk through) topped with shock blocks that electrocute anyone climbing over (easy bots skip this) and keep it stocked, and sappers blast through rock with
explosives and raid the enemy bank to blow a way through its defences. They avoid lava (and flee it when it flows towards them), take diamonds straight home, keep
the teams even and leave to make room when a person joins a full server. The "bots" console command on
its own lists what each bot is doing. "botskill = easy, normal or hard" (or COMPUTER SKILL in HOST GAME,
or the "botskill" command) sets how well they play: easy bots dig and walk slower and notice less; hard
bots dig as fast as the tools allow, notice more and keep at it longer, but never beyond what a player
can do.

Server console commands: help, players, bots <count>, kick <ip or name>, ban <ip or name>, unban <ip>, say <message>,
tp <player> <x> <y> <z>, save <file>, load <file>, restart, quit. "load" sends the new map to everyone.
With "adminpassword" set, players can type "/login <password>" in chat and then run the same commands from
chat (e.g. "/kick Griefer"). The server reads commands from standard input, so it also runs headless (e.g. under
systemd or Docker); Ctrl-C and SIGTERM shut it down cleanly.


WHAT CHANGED IN THE PORT
========================

  XNA 3.0               -> MonoGame 3.8 (DesktopGL: SDL2 + OpenGL + OpenAL)
  .NET Framework 2.0    -> .NET 8
  Lidgren.Network (v1)  -> LiteNetLib (same channels and ordering guarantees)
  Content pipeline      -> PNG/WAV/MP3/TTF files loaded at runtime
  Custom HLSL shaders   -> MonoGame's built-in effects (BasicEffect fog reproduces the distance fade;
                           bloom is done with blend-state passes), so no shader compiler is needed
  WinForms/Win32 calls  -> cross-platform equivalents (window focus, clipboard, error reporting)

New in the port: engineers can build linked teleporters, miners carry a drill that bores through rock,
and servers can enable team home bases (see ROADMAP.md). Otherwise the game rules, world generator,
physics, classes, tools and menus are unchanged. Some fixes were made along the way:

  - The server is authoritative instead of trusting the client (see ROADMAP.md): it checks movement
    (speed, walls, flying) and corrects cheaters, picks respawn points, decides deaths (lava, shock,
    explosions, falls), enforces each class's tools, blocks and cooldowns, only lets you bank next to
    your own bank, and rate-limits chat, pings and sounds. Bad packets are ignored instead of crashing
    the server or other players' games. Corrections are logged as "ANTICHEAT:" on the server console.
  - Map chunks and block updates share one ordered channel, so a block change can no longer be undone
    by a late map chunk while joining.
  - Scores above $65,535 no longer wrap on the scoreboard.
  - Chat and the direct connect box use the system's keyboard layout instead of a hard-coded US layout.
  - Connection errors (version mismatch, banned, server full, disconnects) are shown in the server
    browser instead of a Windows message box.

The network protocol is not compatible with v1.5, so the version is now v1.9.


DEVELOPMENT
===========

    dotnet build src/Infiniminer.sln
    dotnet test src/Infiniminer.sln

The tests cover the shared protocol code and the world generator, and run a real server that a bot client
joins over UDP (map download, team and class selection, digging, building and input validation).

    tests/e2e/smoke.sh      # needs Xvfb, xdotool and ImageMagick

The smoke test plays the real game against a real server under a virtual display, using a scripted
controller (INFINIMINER_TEST_GAMEPAD): joining, choosing a team and class, digging, the in-game menu, a
server restart and automatic rejoin, leaving, and hosting a game. It follows the game through its trace
output (INFINIMINER_TRACE=1) and saves a screenshot of each step to out/e2e. CI runs it on every push.

The game can join a server straight away with "--connect <address>" (or "+connect <address>").


BUILDING THE ORIGINAL VERSION
=============================

Building the original version in code/ requires Visual Studio 2008 and XNA Game Studio 3.0.

You'll also need the fonts 04b03b and 04b08 from the following site:

http://www.dsg4.com/04/extra/bitmap/

Simply build the solution and you should be good to go.


